<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Tippecanoe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1811">1811</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumsehs-war">Tecumseh's War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Tippecanoe was fought on 7 November 1811 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophetstown">Prophetstown</a>, in present-day Indiana, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> forces under Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-henry-harrison">William Henry Harrison</a> and warriors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a>'s confederacy, led in his absence by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenskwatawa">Tenskwatawa</a>. Harrison's army repelled a dawn attack and then burned Prophetstown. The battle damaged the Prophet's prestige and pushed Tecumseh towards alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-tippecanoe.jpg" alt="Battle of Tippecanoe" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kurtz &amp; Allison, Popular Graphic Arts · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Tippecanoe_LCCN2003656861.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-henry-harrison">William Henry Harrison</a> became governor of the newly formed Indiana Territory in 1800 and sought land for settlement. By the 1809 Treaty of Fort Wayne the Miami, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potawatomi">Potawatomi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> and other tribes ceded about 3 million acres to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Tecumseh opposed the treaty, holding that land was owned in common by all tribes and could not be sold without the agreement of all. He built a resistance at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophetstown">Prophetstown</a>, near the meeting of the Tippecanoe and Wabash rivers, while his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenskwatawa">Tenskwatawa</a> preached a return to ancestral ways.</p><p>Meetings with Harrison at Grouseland, his home in Vincennes, in 1810 and again in the summer of 1811 settled nothing. In 1811 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a> left to recruit among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a> and Choctaw. Harrison gathered about 1,000 men, among them Kentucky cavalry volunteers under Joseph Hamilton Daveiss and regulars under Colonel John Parker Boyd, built Fort Harrison at Terre Haute in October and marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophetstown">Prophetstown</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenskwatawa">Tenskwatawa</a> had perhaps 500 warriors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kickapoo">Kickapoo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potawatomi">Potawatomi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chunk">Winnebago</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wyandot">Wyandot</a> and other nations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Harrison camped on Burnett's Creek on 6 November and arranged to meet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenskwatawa">Tenskwatawa</a> the next day. Accounts of how the fighting began differ; Tenskwatawa later blamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chunk">Ho-Chunk</a> warriors, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kickapoo">Kickapoo</a> chief said the shooting of two Winnebago warriors by sentries provoked the attack. Around 4:30 a.m. on 7 November the soldiers woke to find themselves nearly surrounded. The warriors, having lost the element of surprise, attacked in many small, uncoordinated assaults from every side.</p><p>Daveiss was killed leading a counter-charge of dragoons, and Captain Spier Spencer's Indiana Yellow Jackets lost all but one officer before reserves under Captain Robb closed the gap with Ensign John Tipton. After about two hours the warriors ran short of ammunition and withdrew into the woods. Harrison lost 37 killed in action and 25 mortally wounded, with 126 others wounded; historians estimate that as many as 50 warriors were killed and 70 to 80 wounded. On 8 November his men burned the deserted town and 5,000 bushels of corn and beans, and dug up its cemetery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Secretary of War William Eustis asked why Harrison had not fortified his camp. Harrison claimed a decisive victory, but the historian Alfred Cave found no confirmation of that in contemporary reports. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a> partly rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophetstown">Prophetstown</a>, and attacks on settlers increased after the New Madrid earthquakes of December 1811, which many tribes took as a sign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenskwatawa">Tenskwatawa</a>'s powers. In the War of 1812 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a>'s warriors fought beside the British until his death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-thames">Battle of the Thames</a> on 5 October 1813. The Whigs later used the slogan Tippecanoe and Tyler too in Harrison's 1840 presidential campaign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Tippecanoe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In his absence William Henry Harrison destroyed Prophetstown after the <b>Battle of Tippecanoe</b> in 1811.</blockquote><a href="/state/tecumsehs-confederacy">Tecumseh's Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
